From a19ee9dea4d6bcd5a449edcae324ea6dddd87b87 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "Brandon Barclay (AK6MJ)" <ak6mj@w7avm.org>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 22:39:40 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 2/4] Say in the log line whether the browser's mic audio
 reached the radio

In DATA mode the radio takes its modulation from the browser's microphone
stream, so a transmission can be keyed, show normal-looking meters, and
still carry no speech: the browser sent nothing (not started / no mic /
wrong input), or sent digital silence. The log gave no hint which.

For DATA-mode transmissions the [tx] line now adds how many mic frames the
server received and how many it played to the radio during the transmit,
and the peak level of the audio played (dBFS, or SILENT), with a warning
when nothing audible reached the radio:

  ... | browser mic frames: 189 received, 189 sent to the radio | mic peak -2 dBFS
  ... | browser mic frames: 154 received, 154 sent to the radio | mic peak SILENT | WARNING: no mic audio reached the radio

Hand-mic transmissions (DATA off) and servers without an audio bridge get
no mic section, so there are no false warnings.

On an IC-7100 this separated a mic that streamed frames of digital silence
(a Bluetooth headset that had not woken up) from one that worked, which the
meters alone could not.

Tests cover the four cases; the PyAV test (run where PyAV is installed)
checks that the peak and the frame counts come from the audio actually
played. The server tests fail against the previous code.
